course: chinese-street
lesson: 26
title: Red envelopes and New Year wishes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At New Year Lín's aunties hand out hóngbāo — red envelopes with money inside — and every child in the room suddenly remembers their manners.
explain: The rules: take it with both hands (shuāng shǒu jiē), say thank you with a wish, and don't open it in front of the giver (bié dāngmiàn chāi) — in English families you open presents at once, in many Chinese families you wait.
explain: Chinese wishes come in neat sets of four syllables: "gōngxǐ fācái" (wishing you wealth and luck), "shēntǐ jiànkāng" (good health), "wànshì rúyì" (may everything go as you wish), "xuéyè jìnbù" (progress at school, a wish for pupils).
explain: Kids add a cheeky rhyme to gōngxǐ fācái: "hóngbāo ná lái!" — "now hand over the red envelope!" — funny with your own family, greedy with your friend's, so leave it to Lín.
explain: The famous joke: parents keep the yāsuìqián, the New Year money, "safe" for the child — and it never comes back.
fact: "hóngbāo" is a red envelope with money in it, given by elders to children at New Year, birthdays and weddings.
fact: "gōngxǐ fācái" means "wishing you wealth and luck!", the classic New Year wish to grown-ups; the answer is the same wish back.
fact: "hóngbāo ná lái" is a joke rhyme, "hand over the red envelope!", funny with your own close family but greedy-sounding with a friend's family.
fact: "xīnnián hǎo" means Happy New Year, short and warm, easier than xīnnián kuàilè for small children.
fact: "shēntǐ jiànkāng" means "wishing you good health", the best wish for grandparents; to a granny it matters more than money wishes.
fact: "xuéyè jìnbù" means "progress at school", a wish elders give to children; you answer "xièxie" and don't wish it back to elders.
fact: "wànshì rúyì" means "may everything go as you wish", a grand four-word wish to anyone.
fact: "bàinián" means New Year visits to relatives and friends, carrying gifts and wishes, in the days after New Year.
fact: "yāsuìqián" is the New Year money in the red envelope, and many parents keep it "safe" for the child.
fact: "mǎ nián" means the Year of the Horse, which began at Chinese New Year 2026; the Year of the Goat (yáng nián) follows in 2027.
fact: In your own zodiac year, běnmìngnián, some people wear red for luck.
fact: "shuāng shǒu jiē" means "take it with both hands"; both hands show respect for a gift, a cup of tea or a red envelope.
fact: "bié dāngmiàn chāi" means "don't open it in front of them", a parent's whispered reminder about presents and red envelopes.
exercise: Lín's auntie hands you a red envelope with money in it. What is it called? | hóngbāo | chūnlián | dēnglong | zòngzi
exercise: You greet Lín's dad at New Year: "gōngxǐ fācái!". What are you wishing him? | Wealth and luck | A long and healthy life | Good marks at school | A happy birthday
exercise: Lín shouts "gōngxǐ fācái, hóngbāo ná lái!" to his own uncle. What's the second bit? | A joke: "now hand over the red envelope!" | A wish for the uncle's good health all year | A thank-you for the dinner | A goodbye
exercise: Should you shout "hóngbāo ná lái" to Lín's grandad on your first visit? | No, from a guest it sounds greedy | Yes, it's the polite thing for guests | Yes, then he'll give you two envelopes | No, it's a swear word
exercise: Lín's 4-year-old sister says a short, easy New Year wish to the neighbours. Which one? | xīnnián hǎo | wànshì rúyì | gōngxǐ fācái | xuéyè jìnbù
exercise: What is the best New Year wish for Lín's granny? | shēntǐ jiànkāng | xuéyè jìnbù | hóngbāo ná lái | xiǎo pàngzi
exercise: Lín's granny wishes you "xuéyè jìnbù". What do you say? | xièxie! | xuéyè jìnbù to you too! | hóngbāo ná lái! | bú yào le!
exercise: Which wish means "may everything go as you wish"? | wànshì rúyì | shēntǐ jiànkāng | xīnnián hǎo | gōngxǐ fācái
exercise: Lín's family spends the week after New Year visiting relatives with gifts. What is it called? | bàinián | guònián | shǒusuì | shǎng yuè
exercise: Lín moans that his yāsuìqián has gone to his mum. What is yāsuìqián? | The New Year money in the red envelope | His weekly pocket money for sweets | A New Year cake | A family photo taken at New Year
exercise: Which year began at Chinese New Year 2026? | The Year of the Horse, mǎ nián | The Year of the Dragon, lóng nián | The Year of the Goat, yáng nián | The Year of the Monkey, hóu nián
exercise: Lín's uncle was born in a Horse year and wears red socks all through 2026. Why? | It's his own zodiac year; red for luck | He lost all his other socks in the wash | Red socks are the uniform at his work | He supports a football team
exercise: Lín's mum whispers "shuāng shǒu jiē" as Granny holds out an envelope. What must you do? | Take it with both hands | Take it with one hand | Bow and refuse it | Open it at once
exercise: Lín's mum whispers "bié dāngmiàn chāi". What does she mean? | Don't open it in front of them | Don't lose it on the way back home | Don't spend it all at once on sweets | Don't show it to your little sister
exercise: How do you receive a cup of tea from Lín's grandad? | With both hands | With one hand, quickly | With your left hand only | Let it be put on the floor
test: my mate says u should rip the hóngbāo open straight away and say "ooh how much". good manners? | No, both hands, thank them, open it later | Yes, opening it at once shows you like it | Yes, and count it out loud for everyone | No, you should give it back
test: Lín's mum says she'll "keep the yāsuìqián safe" for him. Lín sighs. Why do families laugh about this? | It's a famous joke: the money never comes back | Because mums always spend it on sweets for themselves | Because the money is fake | Because Lín will get double later
test: You wish Lín's granny "xuéyè jìnbù!". She laughs kindly. Why? | It's a wish for pupils, not for elders | It's a rude word for old people | It means "go to sleep now" | It's only for weddings
test: Granny gets two wishes: "gōngxǐ fācái" from one child and "shēntǐ jiànkāng" from you. Which does she probably love more? | shēntǐ jiànkāng — health matters most to her | gōngxǐ fācái — she really wants to get rich | Neither, wishes are rude to grannies | Both are wrong for grannies
test: In a cartoon a boy grabs a red envelope with one hand and tears it open. Lín's mum tuts. Which two rules did he break? | Both hands, and not opening it in front of them | Saying sorry first and saying goodbye after | Wearing red and bowing | Eating first and singing
test: It's 2027. Lín's dad says "jīnnián shì mǎ nián". Is he right? | No, 2027 is the Year of the Goat | Yes, the Horse lasts for two years | Yes, every single year is the Horse | No, 2027 is the Year of the Dragon
test: What would happen if you said nothing and stuffed the hóngbāo in your pocket? | It'd look careless; say thank you with a wish | Everyone would clap for being so quick | You'd get another one | Nothing, no one cares about that
test: Lín's mum: "we're going to bàinián at Auntie Li's". What will you do there? | Visit with gifts and New Year wishes | Watch the full moon from her garden | Race dragon boats | Clean the graves of the family
test: Which wish is four neat syllables meaning "may everything go as you wish"? | wànshì rúyì | xīnnián hǎo | hóngbāo | bié dāngmiàn chāi
test: Lín's little cousin says "gōngxǐ fācái, hóngbāo ná lái!" to his own grandad, who roars with laughter. Rude? | No, within close family it's a loved joke | Yes, the worst insult you can give a grandad | Yes, he'll get nothing | No, it means "happy birthday"
test: Your English granny gives Lín a present and he doesn't open it. She thinks he doesn't like it. What's the truth? | In many Chinese families you open gifts later | He really didn't like it, he was just being polite | He forgot it was there | He's going to give it back to her
exam: Identify the customary mode of receiving a red envelope from an elder. | With both hands, accompanied by thanks and a wish | With one hand, in silence and without looking | By declining it thrice and departing | By opening it at once and counting the contents
exam: It is given that the wish "xuéyè jìnbù" signifies progress in one's studies. Select the statement that accurately characterises its proper use. | It is bestowed by elders upon pupils, not returned to elders | It is addressed by pupils to their grandparents as a mark of respect | It is reserved for weddings | It is a request for money
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phrase "hóngbāo ná lái"? | A jocular rhyme demanding the envelope, apt only within close family | A solemn blessing for the health of elders, uttered on New Year's Eve | A formal expression of gratitude | A prohibition on opening gifts
exam: Ascertain the zodiac animal of the year commencing at the Chinese New Year of 2026. | The Horse | The Goat | The Dragon | The Rat
